<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's the Rainy Season</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need an Umbrella.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Relay the teacher's instructions during group activities. concept: Explain steps in an experiment to peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can relay teacher's instructions about rainy day activities using basic gestures (e.g., points to clouds and says, 'Draw clouds.'). Can explain steps in an experiment by demonstrating actions (e.g., shows pouring water for 'This is rain.'). Can help peers understand rainy season concepts by using simple words and gestures (e.g., pointing to rain and saying, 'Rain is water.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of rainy day items (umbrella, raincoat, boots, puddle). Teacher asks, "What do you need when it's rainy?" and "What do you wear in the rain?" Students respond chorally or individually, using "I need an umbrella." or "I wear my boots." Teacher then points to a flashcard of a cloud and asks, "What is this?" and a picture of rain and asks, "What is happening here?" to elicit "cloud" and "It is raining." Focus on /r/ in 'rain'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to give a simple instruction and how to relay it. For example, the teacher says, "Draw a cloud." and gestures. Then, the teacher turns to one student and says, "Tell your friend 'Draw a cloud.'" The teacher models for the student how to turn to another peer, point, and say, "Draw a cloud." The teacher also models demonstrating actions for concepts, like pretending to pour water and saying, "This is rain. We pour water."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Wear your raincoat,' what do you do?" (Expected: Students point to their imagined raincoat or mime putting it on).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I tell you, 'Tell your friend to open the book,' what do you say to your friend?" (Expected: Students say, "Open the book!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's the Rainy Season")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, illustrated step-by-step instructions (diagrams with simple sentences) for a "Rain Cloud in a Jar" experiment. Each step uses simple present tense verbs and target vocabulary (e.g., "First, pour water into the jar. Next, put shaving cream on top. Then, drop blue food coloring. See the rain drop!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Narrating current events related to weather (by explaining the process of 'making rain').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows the instructions one by one, reading aloud and gesturing. "What happens first?" "What do we pour?" (Expected: "Water!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the cloud made of?" (Expected: "Shaving cream!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you see coming down?" (Expected: "Rain!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks students to point to the picture that shows "pour water" or "rain drop."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Relay teacher's instructions; Explain steps in an experiment. Using Present simple, Present continuous, WH-questions, and vocabulary: pour, drop, wet, cloud, sky, water, rain, puddle, umbrella, boots.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Sequencing the Rain Cloud"</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive scrambled picture cards of the "Rain Cloud in a Jar" experiment steps. In pairs, they order the pictures and then orally say the steps using simple sentences (e.g., "First, we pour water. Then, we add shaving cream."). The teacher circulates, checking for accuracy in sequencing and pronunciation of /r/ in 'rain' and /p/ in 'pour'.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Relay the Action"</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students sit in small groups. The teacher gives an instruction to the first student in each group (e.g., "Point to the sky," "Pretend to wear a raincoat," "Show me a puddle-jump"). That student then relays the instruction to the next student in their group, who performs the action. The relay continues down the line. Focus is on clear relaying and correct action. Teacher observes for simple vocabulary and gestures.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check: "Explain a Rainy Day"</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. Student A describes an activity they do on a rainy day (from their "Rainy Day Journal" project, e.g., "It is raining. I am reading a book inside."). Student B then asks a simple WH-question for clarification (e.g., "What book are you reading?", "Where are you reading?"). Student A explains or clarifies, using simple words and gestures. Then they switch roles. Teacher prompts with vocabulary like 'wet,' 'gloomy,' 'muddy.' Phonemic awareness: teacher listens for correct spelling of 'raincoat' or 'muddy' if students write briefly, and blending /sk/ /aɪ/ in 'sky'.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's the Rainy Season")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group explanation and peer clarification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "My Rainy Day Journal & Experiment Explanation"</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. Each student brings their "Rainy Day Journal" entry (a drawing or simple sentence describing a rainy day activity, e.g., "It's raining today. I am playing a board game inside.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student (the 'explainer') presents their journal entry to the group, saying, "It's raining today. I am (activity). I like (activity)." and shows their picture. They use present continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Another student (the 'mediator') in the group is designated to *relay* or *explain* what the explainer said to another peer in the group, or to clarify if someone didn't understand. For example, if a peer looks confused, the mediator might point to the drawing and say, "He is playing. Inside. Not wet." or "It is raining now. She needs an umbrella."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Additionally, each group leader (a designated student) must clearly explain one step of the "Rain Cloud in a Jar" experiment to their group using simple words and gestures. They demonstrate the action as they explain, e.g., "First, pour water." The other students try to understand.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing students' ability to narrate, ask clarifying WH-questions, and mediate information for peers using simple language and gestures.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> In their small groups, students take turns explaining their Rainy Day Journal entry to a peer. The teacher then gives a simple instruction to one student (e.g., "Tell your friend to draw a cloud.") and observes if the student can accurately relay it. Another task involves a student explaining one specific step of the "Rain Cloud in a Jar" experiment to a peer, demonstrating with gestures, and responding to a peer's simple clarifying question (e.g., "Why wet?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear /r/ in 'rain' and /p/ in 'puddle/pour'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (puddle, umbrella, raincoat, boots, cloud, sky, pour, drop, wet, muddy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of explanation/relayed instructions:</b> Uses simple sentences (Present simple, Present continuous) and gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Helps peers understand concepts (e.g., pointing to rain and saying, "Rain is water.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write or draw their answers to the following:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to explain today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn about helping your friends understand?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you use gestures to help your friends understand? Show me."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or hard to explain the rainy day activity/experiment? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well during the mediation activities? (e.g., Students effectively used gestures when explaining, the visual aids for the experiment helped relay instructions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students to better relay instructions or explain concepts to peers in the future? (e.g., More explicit modeling of sentence starters for explanations, pair practice with specific feedback on clarity).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "I Need an Umbrella." theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, building on the communicative skills developed in this sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>